1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:10,291
On the twenty-sixth of December, nineteen hundred, a small steamer called the Hesperus came around the western edge of Scotland and found a lighthouse with nobody in it.

2
00:00:10,991 --> 00:00:12,969
There was no flag on the pole.

3
00:00:13,289 --> 00:00:16,552
There were no empty supply crates waiting at the landing.

4
00:00:16,872 --> 00:00:19,545
Nobody came down the steps to catch a rope.

5
00:00:19,865 --> 00:00:22,168
The ship's captain sounded his whistle.

6
00:00:22,488 --> 00:00:24,689
Then he fired a signal rocket.

7
00:00:25,009 --> 00:00:27,250
The island answered with silence.

8
00:00:27,950 --> 00:00:30,435
Three men had been living on that rock.

9
00:00:30,755 --> 00:00:32,446
They were never seen again.

10
00:00:34,296 --> 00:00:38,173
This is one of the most retold mysteries in British history.

11
00:00:38,493 --> 00:00:40,881
And that is exactly the problem.

12
00:00:41,201 --> 00:00:51,967
Over a hundred and twenty years, the story has picked up a ghost ship, a sea serpent, an overturned chair, a meal left half eaten, and a logbook full of weeping men and prayers.

13
00:00:52,667 --> 00:00:54,610
Most of that never happened.

14
00:00:55,310 --> 00:00:58,500
So tonight we are going to do something a little different.

15
00:00:58,820 --> 00:01:04,057
We are going to tell the story using only what the people who were actually there wrote down.

16
00:01:04,377 --> 00:01:08,789
And at the end, we will separate what is documented from what is legend.

17
00:01:09,109 --> 00:01:14,350
Because the real version is stranger, and sadder, than the one you have probably heard.

18
00:01:16,200 --> 00:01:17,653
Start with the place.

19
00:01:17,973 --> 00:01:27,370
The Flannan Isles are a scatter of small, steep islands in the Atlantic, roughly twenty miles west of the Isle of Lewis in the Outer Hebrides.

20
00:01:27,690 --> 00:01:30,051
Sailors called them the Seven Hunters.

21
00:01:30,371 --> 00:01:34,375
The largest is Eilean Mòr, which simply means the big island.

22
00:01:34,695 --> 00:01:36,270
Big is relative.

23
00:01:36,590 --> 00:01:38,878
You can walk across it in a few minutes.

24
00:01:39,578 --> 00:01:41,870
It has no trees and no harbour.

25
00:01:42,190 --> 00:01:47,869
The sides are cliffs, and the sea that hits them has come all the way across the open ocean.

26
00:01:48,189 --> 00:02:00,471
For centuries the only people who went there were shepherds bringing sheep over for summer grazing, and the occasional visitor to the ruins of a tiny chapel said to be connected to a seventh century saint.

27
00:02:00,791 --> 00:02:05,480
Local tradition treated the island with respect bordering on unease.

28
00:02:05,800 --> 00:02:10,376
People who stayed there overnight were expected to observe certain customs.

29
00:02:10,696 --> 00:02:12,961
It was not a place you went casually.

30
00:02:14,811 --> 00:02:18,131
But ships were going past it, and ships were being lost.

31
00:02:18,451 --> 00:02:25,552
So in the eighteen nineties the Northern Lighthouse Board decided to build a light on the summit of Eilean Mòr.

32
00:02:25,872 --> 00:02:32,373
The design came from David Alan Stevenson, of the famous family of Scottish lighthouse engineers.

33
00:02:32,693 --> 00:02:44,196
The construction took four years, because every stone, every tool and every bag of cement had to be landed on a rock with no beach, by men working on narrow steps cut into the cliff.

34
00:02:44,896 --> 00:02:54,850
The builders made two landing places, one on the east side of the island and one on the west, so that a boat could come in whichever side was sheltered from the weather.

35
00:02:55,170 --> 00:03:00,214
From each landing, steps and a small railway climbed to the lighthouse on top.

36
00:03:00,914 --> 00:03:05,242
The light was first lit on the seventh of December, eighteen ninety nine.

37
00:03:05,562 --> 00:03:09,596
It had been working for almost exactly one year when it went dark.

38
00:03:11,446 --> 00:03:15,122
The station was run by a rotating crew of four keepers.

39
00:03:15,442 --> 00:03:19,435
At any time, three were on the island and one was ashore on leave.

40
00:03:19,755 --> 00:03:33,025
In December nineteen hundred, the three men on the rock were James Ducat, the principal keeper, Thomas Marshall, his assistant, and Donald McArthur, who was the occasional keeper, filling in for the man on leave.

41
00:03:33,725 --> 00:03:35,547
These were not adventurers.

42
00:03:35,867 --> 00:03:42,807
Lighthouse keeping was a job with a rulebook, and the Northern Lighthouse Board took the rulebook very seriously.

43
00:03:43,127 --> 00:03:47,281
The lamp had to be lit at sunset and kept burning until sunrise.

44
00:03:47,601 --> 00:03:51,564
The lens had to be cleaned, the wicks trimmed, the oil topped up.

45
00:03:51,884 --> 00:03:55,347
Every day's weather and work went into the station records.

46
00:03:55,667 --> 00:04:01,259
And one rule mattered more than all the others: the light must never be left unattended.

47
00:04:01,959 --> 00:04:04,558
Ducat had a wife and four children.

48
00:04:04,878 --> 00:04:07,340
McArthur had a wife and two children.

49
00:04:07,660 --> 00:04:13,273
They had been chosen by a superintendent named Robert Muirhead, who knew all of them personally.

50
00:04:13,593 --> 00:04:15,295
Hold on to that name.

51
00:04:15,615 --> 00:04:18,411
He is the reason we know anything at all.

52
00:04:20,261 --> 00:04:25,837
The first sign that something was wrong came from a ship that had nothing to do with the lighthouse.

53
00:04:26,537 --> 00:04:32,706
On the night of the fifteenth of December, a steamer called the Archtor was heading from Philadelphia to Leith.

54
00:04:33,026 --> 00:04:38,139
As it passed the Flannan Isles in poor weather, its crew noticed that there was no light.

55
00:04:38,459 --> 00:04:40,625
The Archtor's log recorded it.

56
00:04:41,325 --> 00:04:48,861
But the Archtor did not reach port until the eighteenth, and the report that the Flannan light was out did not move quickly.

57
00:04:49,181 --> 00:04:51,716
Nobody launched an emergency mission.

58
00:04:52,036 --> 00:04:55,202
The island had no telegraph and no radio.

59
00:04:55,522 --> 00:05:01,813
Communication with the keepers depended on the relief ship, and the relief ship was already scheduled.

60
00:05:03,663 --> 00:05:10,713
The Hesperus was due to visit around the twentieth of December, bringing supplies and the keeper returning from leave.

61
00:05:11,033 --> 00:05:12,884
That man was Joseph Moore.

62
00:05:13,204 --> 00:05:17,171
But the weather in the Minch was bad, and the Hesperus could not sail.

63
00:05:17,490 --> 00:05:21,887
It waited at port on Lewis, day after day, for a gap in the weather.

64
00:05:22,587 --> 00:05:25,113
It did not get out until the twenty-sixth.

65
00:05:25,433 --> 00:05:26,810
Boxing Day.

66
00:05:27,510 --> 00:05:31,990
So for eleven days, from the fifteenth to the twenty-sixth, nobody knew.

67
00:05:32,310 --> 00:05:41,835
A light that was supposed to warn ships off the rocks sat dark at the edge of the Atlantic, and the only people who might have explained why were already gone.

68
00:05:43,685 --> 00:05:50,196
When the Hesperus finally arrived, around midday, the captain, Jim Harvie, brought her in to the landing.

69
00:05:50,516 --> 00:05:53,726
You already know what he saw, or rather what he didn't.

70
00:05:54,046 --> 00:05:55,351
No flag.

71
00:05:55,671 --> 00:05:56,987
No crates.

72
00:05:57,307 --> 00:05:58,643
No keepers.

73
00:05:59,343 --> 00:06:03,405
Joseph Moore went ashore alone and climbed the steps to the lighthouse.

74
00:06:05,255 --> 00:06:13,623
Moore later wrote down exactly what he found, and his account is worth hearing carefully, because almost every later version changes it.

75
00:06:14,323 --> 00:06:16,241
The entrance gate was closed.

76
00:06:16,561 --> 00:06:18,337
The main door was closed.

77
00:06:18,656 --> 00:06:22,378
Inside, the kitchen fire had not been lit for some time.

78
00:06:22,698 --> 00:06:25,273
The beds had not been slept in recently.

79
00:06:25,593 --> 00:06:29,319
The clock on the wall had stopped, because nobody had wound it.

80
00:06:30,019 --> 00:06:34,214
In the lamp room, the lamp itself had been cleaned and the oil filled.

81
00:06:34,534 --> 00:06:36,512
The station work had been done.

82
00:06:36,832 --> 00:06:38,864
That tells us something important.

83
00:06:39,184 --> 00:06:43,909
The keepers had finished their routine for the day before whatever happened, happened.

84
00:06:44,229 --> 00:06:46,670
They had prepared the light to be lit at dusk.

85
00:06:46,990 --> 00:06:48,506
Then nobody lit it.

86
00:06:49,206 --> 00:06:53,077
And there was one detail that would matter more than anything else.

87
00:06:53,397 --> 00:06:59,914
In the lobby hung the keepers' oilskins, the heavy waterproof coats they wore to go outside in bad weather.

88
00:07:00,234 --> 00:07:01,788
Two sets were missing.

89
00:07:02,108 --> 00:07:04,011
One set was still on its hook.

90
00:07:04,711 --> 00:07:08,200
Moore searched the station and the island and found no one.

91
00:07:08,520 --> 00:07:11,391
He went back down to the boat and told the captain.

92
00:07:13,241 --> 00:07:20,665
Harvie left Moore and three volunteer sailors on the island to keep the light going, and took the Hesperus back to Lewis.

93
00:07:20,985 --> 00:07:25,662
From there he sent a telegram to the Northern Lighthouse Board in Edinburgh.

94
00:07:26,362 --> 00:07:31,204
Paraphrased, it said this: a dreadful accident has happened at the Flannans.

95
00:07:31,524 --> 00:07:34,695
The three keepers have disappeared from the island.

96
00:07:35,015 --> 00:07:40,616
The clocks were stopped and other signs indicate the accident must have happened about a week ago.

97
00:07:40,936 --> 00:07:46,318
Poor fellows, they must have been blown over the cliffs or drowned trying to secure a crane.

98
00:07:47,018 --> 00:07:51,916
Notice that, even on the first day, the captain's instinct was not a ghost.

99
00:07:52,236 --> 00:07:53,575
It was the sea.

100
00:07:55,425 --> 00:08:02,266
On the twenty-ninth of December, Robert Muirhead arrived on Eilean Mòr to lead the official investigation.

101
00:08:02,586 --> 00:08:05,148
He was the man who had hired these keepers.

102
00:08:05,468 --> 00:08:07,031
He knew their faces.

103
00:08:07,351 --> 00:08:13,860
His report, submitted on the eighth of January nineteen oh one, is the closest thing we have to an answer.

104
00:08:14,560 --> 00:08:18,052
Muirhead started where Moore had, with the station records.

105
00:08:18,372 --> 00:08:25,035
They showed the keepers had carried out their normal duties into the fifteenth of December, and nothing unusual.

106
00:08:25,355 --> 00:08:33,831
Whatever took the men had come on the fifteenth, after the day's work and after their midday meal, and before the light was due to be lit at dusk.

107
00:08:34,151 --> 00:08:38,456
That fits perfectly with what the Archtor saw that night: darkness.

108
00:08:40,306 --> 00:08:43,184
Then Muirhead walked down to the west landing.

109
00:08:43,504 --> 00:08:48,125
And this is where the story stops being mysterious, and starts being frightening.

110
00:08:48,825 --> 00:08:51,141
The east landing was undamaged.

111
00:08:51,461 --> 00:08:53,462
The west landing had been wrecked.

112
00:08:54,162 --> 00:09:02,800
There was a box at the west landing, set into a crevice in the rock about thirty three metres above sea level, around a hundred and ten feet up.

113
00:09:03,120 --> 00:09:05,611
It held mooring ropes and landing gear.

114
00:09:05,931 --> 00:09:09,110
The box had been broken and its contents were gone.

115
00:09:09,430 --> 00:09:11,950
Iron railings had been bent out of shape.

116
00:09:12,270 --> 00:09:16,035
A section of the railway track had been torn out of its concrete.

117
00:09:16,355 --> 00:09:20,275
A boulder weighing more than a ton had been moved from where it sat.

118
00:09:20,595 --> 00:09:28,458
And up on the cliff top, more than sixty metres above the water, the turf had been ripped away as far as ten metres back from the edge.

119
00:09:29,158 --> 00:09:34,797
Muirhead wrote that he would not have believed the extent of the damage if he had not seen it himself.

120
00:09:36,647 --> 00:09:38,830
Put those facts next to each other.

121
00:09:39,150 --> 00:09:49,016
A storm had hit the west side of the island hard enough to tear out ironwork and move a one ton rock, at heights most people would consider completely safe.

122
00:09:49,336 --> 00:09:53,662
The rope box, a hundred and ten feet above the sea, had been smashed.

123
00:09:54,362 --> 00:09:56,436
Now go back to the oilskins.

124
00:09:56,756 --> 00:09:58,264
Two sets missing.

125
00:09:58,584 --> 00:10:00,190
One left behind.

126
00:10:00,890 --> 00:10:03,333
Muirhead's reconstruction went like this.

127
00:10:03,653 --> 00:10:15,046
On the afternoon of the fifteenth, Ducat and Marshall put on their oilskins and went down to the west landing, probably to secure the rope box and the equipment after the weather had been rough.

128
00:10:15,366 --> 00:10:21,784
A keeper who lost equipment to the sea could be fined, and at least one of these men had been fined before.

129
00:10:22,104 --> 00:10:26,062
McArthur stayed at the station, because someone always had to.

130
00:10:26,762 --> 00:10:29,892
At some point, McArthur left the station too.

131
00:10:30,212 --> 00:10:35,101
He left in a hurry, in his shirtsleeves, without his oilskins, in heavy rain.

132
00:10:35,421 --> 00:10:37,584
Breaking the one rule that mattered.

133
00:10:37,904 --> 00:10:44,842
Muirhead noted, with an investigator's dryness, that whoever was the last to leave had broken the regulations.

134
00:10:45,542 --> 00:10:48,138
Why would a careful keeper run out like that?

135
00:10:48,458 --> 00:10:52,480
The most likely answer is that he saw something from the station.

136
00:10:52,800 --> 00:10:55,272
A huge sea coming in at the west side.

137
00:10:55,592 --> 00:10:57,604
Or the other two men in trouble.

138
00:10:58,304 --> 00:11:05,519
And then a wave, far larger than anything those men expected, came up the cliff, over the landing, and took all three.

139
00:11:07,369 --> 00:11:09,603
No bodies were ever recovered.

140
00:11:09,923 --> 00:11:12,417
That is not unusual for the Atlantic.

141
00:11:12,737 --> 00:11:17,741
The sea around the Flannans is deep and violent, and the currents go a long way.

142
00:11:18,441 --> 00:11:24,051
Muirhead's verdict was that the men had been swept away by an extraordinarily large sea.

143
00:11:24,371 --> 00:11:35,804
It is the conclusion of the man who knew the station and the men better than anyone, who stood on the damaged landing a fortnight after the event, and who had every reason to want a different answer.

144
00:11:36,124 --> 00:11:37,378
He found none.

145
00:11:39,228 --> 00:11:44,147
So if the official explanation is that clear, why is this still called a mystery?

146
00:11:44,847 --> 00:11:48,562
Because over the following decades, the story was rewritten.

147
00:11:48,882 --> 00:11:52,111
And the rewritten version is the one most people know.

148
00:11:53,961 --> 00:12:00,987
The best known piece of the legend is a set of logbook entries said to have been written by the keepers in their last days.

149
00:12:01,307 --> 00:12:04,910
In these entries, a terrible storm rages for days.

150
00:12:05,230 --> 00:12:07,695
One keeper is described as crying.

151
00:12:08,015 --> 00:12:11,016
Another is said to be irritable, or silent.

152
00:12:11,336 --> 00:12:12,774
The men are praying.

153
00:12:13,094 --> 00:12:18,609
And the final line reads something like: storm ended, sea calm, God is over all.

154
00:12:19,309 --> 00:12:20,715
It is haunting.

155
00:12:21,035 --> 00:12:22,701
It is also fiction.

156
00:12:23,021 --> 00:12:31,017
The researcher Mike Dash, writing for Fortean Times, traced these entries and showed that they do not come from the real records.

157
00:12:31,337 --> 00:12:33,412
They were added to the story later.

158
00:12:33,732 --> 00:12:38,754
The real station records contain routine entries, not confessions of fear.

159
00:12:39,074 --> 00:12:50,224
And there is another problem: there were no reports of a great storm in the area on the twelfth to the fourteenth of December, the days when those dramatic entries are supposed to have been written.

160
00:12:52,074 --> 00:12:54,378
The second layer came from poetry.

161
00:12:54,698 --> 00:13:02,643
In nineteen twelve, Wilfrid Wilson Gibson published a ballad called Flannan Isle, told from the point of view of the relief crew.

162
00:13:02,963 --> 00:13:04,559
It is a good poem.

163
00:13:04,879 --> 00:13:14,465
But in it, the men find a table set for a meal that has not been eaten, and a chair knocked over, as if the keepers had leapt up in terror and fled mid bite.

164
00:13:15,165 --> 00:13:19,015
That image was so vivid that it became part of the history.

165
00:13:19,335 --> 00:13:22,070
But it is not what Joseph Moore reported.

166
00:13:22,390 --> 00:13:24,853
By his account, the kitchen was clean.

167
00:13:25,173 --> 00:13:27,627
The meal had been eaten and cleared away.

168
00:13:27,947 --> 00:13:34,949
Which is exactly what you would expect if three men finished dinner, went out to deal with the weather, and never came back.

169
00:13:36,799 --> 00:13:39,050
After that, the theories piled up.

170
00:13:39,370 --> 00:13:41,076
A giant sea creature.

171
00:13:41,396 --> 00:13:44,044
Foreign agents who kidnapped the keepers.

172
00:13:44,364 --> 00:13:49,657
A phantom boat crewed by the dead, drawn from local stories about the Seven Hunters.

173
00:13:49,977 --> 00:13:54,744
A plan by the three men to fake their deaths and start new lives somewhere else.

174
00:13:55,444 --> 00:13:58,424
None of these has any evidence behind it at all.

175
00:13:58,744 --> 00:14:06,557
They exist because the legend left a gap, a table set for a meal nobody ate, and imagination rushed in to fill it.

176
00:14:08,407 --> 00:14:10,551
There are more grounded variations.

177
00:14:10,871 --> 00:14:19,461
One researcher has suggested that Ducat and Marshall went to save their equipment, and McArthur was lost trying to warn or help them.

178
00:14:19,781 --> 00:14:28,202
A man who kept the Flannan light in the nineteen fifties believed that one keeper was swept off and the other two were lost trying to rescue him.

179
00:14:28,522 --> 00:14:33,500
Both of these are really refinements of Muirhead's account, not alternatives to it.

180
00:14:33,820 --> 00:14:44,533
There is also a darker theory, that a quarrel turned violent near the cliff edge, but it leans on gossip about one man's temper rather than on anything found on the island.

181
00:14:46,383 --> 00:14:48,193
So here is where we land.

182
00:14:48,893 --> 00:14:54,248
Documented: the light went out on the night of the fifteenth of December, nineteen hundred.

183
00:14:54,568 --> 00:14:57,047
The station work had been finished that day.

184
00:14:57,367 --> 00:15:00,640
Two sets of oilskins were gone and one was left.

185
00:15:00,960 --> 00:15:05,556
The west landing showed severe storm damage at heights over a hundred feet.

186
00:15:05,876 --> 00:15:10,063
Three experienced men disappeared, and the sea never gave them back.

187
00:15:10,763 --> 00:15:13,150
Legend: the dramatic logbook.

188
00:15:13,470 --> 00:15:16,211
The uneaten meal and the overturned chair.

189
00:15:16,531 --> 00:15:19,360
The sea serpent, the spies, the ghost ship.

190
00:15:21,210 --> 00:15:24,603
What is left is not really a mystery about what happened.

191
00:15:24,923 --> 00:15:27,632
It is a mystery about the last few minutes.

192
00:15:27,952 --> 00:15:29,636
Who saw the wave first.

193
00:15:29,956 --> 00:15:33,653
Whether McArthur ran out to warn the others, or to help them.

194
00:15:33,973 --> 00:15:42,113
What the three of them said to each other on the steps of the west landing, a hundred feet above a sea that should never have been able to reach them.

195
00:15:42,813 --> 00:15:45,071
Nobody will ever know that part.

196
00:15:45,391 --> 00:15:51,116
The Flannan light was automated in nineteen seventy one, and there have been no keepers there since.

197
00:15:51,436 --> 00:15:53,565
The station records survived.

198
00:15:53,885 --> 00:15:55,248
The men did not.

199
00:15:55,948 --> 00:16:03,498
The full list of sources for this story is in the description, along with the line between what we know and what was added later.

200
00:16:03,818 --> 00:16:12,245
If there is a story you think history left unfinished, tell me in the comments, and subscribe if you want more true stories like this one.

201
00:16:12,565 --> 00:16:14,528
This is The Quiet Archive.
